EM="Ch 20 Embryology"; OB="Ch 40 Obstruction pathophysiology"; PU="Ch 42 Paediatric ureter surgery"; SK="Ch 59 Genital skin diseases"; UO="Ch 89 Upper tract obstruction"; NM="Ch 122 Neuromodulation"; RP="Ch 155 Open radical prostatectomy"
R=[
["Kidney development","Which statement about kidney development is FALSE?",
 ["Nephrogenesis goes on for two years after birth","All three kidneys come from intermediate mesoderm","The oldest nephrons lie deep, near the medulla","Nephrons come from mesenchyme, ducts from bud"],0,
 "Kidneys appear in order: pronephros, mesonephros, metanephros; the first two regress; all come from intermediate mesoderm. The metanephros forms in the sacral region when the ureteric bud sprouts from the distal nephric duct and meets metanephric mesenchyme (book: about 4 weeks). Glomerulus, proximal tubule, loop of Henle and distal tubule come from metanephric mesenchyme; collecting ducts, calyces, pelvis and ureter from the bud. Older, more mature nephrons are inner (juxtamedullary). Nephrogenesis is completed before birth; maturation continues postnatally.",EM,["c020-a01","c020-r17","c020-a02","c020-a03","c020-r18","c020-r01","c020-r02"]],
["Fetal obstruction","Which statement contrasting congenital with acquired obstruction is FALSE?",
 ["Congenital obstruction shows heavy inflammation","Only congenital obstruction alters glomeruli","Acquired obstruction shows lymphocyte infiltrate","The developing kidney may never fully recover"],0,
 "Congenital obstruction lacks a significant inflammatory infiltrate unless infected; postnatal acquired obstruction commonly shows interstitial lymphocytes. Only congenital obstruction alters glomerular development; acquired obstruction can only damage an established pattern. Key features of congenital obstruction: altered growth regulation, differentiation and functional integration (with interstitial fibrosis, tubular hypotrophy, more epithelial–mesenchymal transformation). Effects in the developing kidney may be irreversible.",OB,["c040-a01","c040-a07","c040-a09","c040-r15","c040-r17","c040-r11"]],
["Steroids in skin disease","Which statement about corticosteroids in genital dermatology is FALSE?",
 ["Split daily doses limit adrenal suppression","Oral steroid effects last 8 to 48 hours","Topical steroids can be absorbed systemically","Peak blood levels occur 30–90 min after a dose"],0,
 "For short courses (≤3 weeks, e.g., allergic contact dermatitis) give a single morning dose to minimise hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal suppression. Oral steroids are absorbed in the jejunum, peak at 30–90 min, have plasma half-lives of 1–5 h but act for 8–48 h. Topical steroids can be absorbed with systemic effects. Periodic acid–Schiff (PAS) staining of skin scrapings identifies fungi such as Candida and dermatophytes.",SK,["c059-a01","c059-a02","c059-a03","c059-r01"]],
["UPJ diagnosis","Which statement about diagnosing ureteropelvic junction (UPJ) obstruction is FALSE?",
 ["MCDK cysts connect with one another","Whitaker perfusion runs at 10 mL per min","Diuretic renography quantifies drainage","Most new cases follow antenatal scans"],0,
 "Maternal ultrasonography detects most asymptomatic newborn hydronephrosis. Diuretic renography assesses drainage and function; US, CT and retrograde pyelography are only anatomical. Whitaker test (via nephrostomy, 10 mL/min): <15 cmH2O non-obstructed, >22 cmH2O obstructed, between = indeterminate; ideal when a nephrostomy is already in place. On US, hydronephrotic 'cysts' connect; MCDK cysts are separate.",UO,["c089-a01","c089-a02","c089-a03","c089-r05","c089-r04"]],
["SNM indications","Which statement about sacral neuromodulation (SNM) is FALSE?",
 ["It is approved for neurogenic bladder","Atonic diabetic bladders respond poorly","Mild MS with no MRI needs may suit it","Use in children is limited by growth data"],0,
 "SNM is an established third-line option for idiopathic bladder dysfunction: FDA-approved for idiopathic OAB and nonobstructive retention (also faecal incontinence), not for bladder pain/interstitial cystitis or neurogenic bladder (IC benefit reported; may expand). The end organ must work: an areflexic diabetic bladder with retention responds poorly. Best MS candidate: mild symptoms, good mobility, no future MRI need. Children: off-label; limited by lack of data on spinal growth and lead position; noninvasive therapies need repeated sessions.",NM,["c122-a01","c122-a02","c122-a07","c122-a09","c122-r07"]],
["Arterial anatomy","Which statement about arterial anatomy at radical prostatectomy is FALSE?",
 ["Accessory pudendal arteries are best divided","The inferior vesical artery feeds the prostate","Capsular vessels mark the cavernous nerves","Aberrant pudendals may arise from obturator"],0,
 "The prostate is supplied by the inferior vesical artery. Capsular arteries and veins run posterolaterally in the lateral pelvic fascia, wrapped in nerves: the macroscopic landmark for the cavernous nerves. The corpora are mainly supplied by the internal pudendal artery, but aberrant/accessory pudendals can arise from obturator, inferior vesical or superior vesical arteries; they run on the anterolateral prostate and should be preserved, especially in older men with borderline penile flow.",RP,["c155-a01","c155-a02","c155-a03","c155-r18","c155-r22"]],
["Renal developmental genes","Which statement about renal developmental genes is TRUE?",
 ["Pax-2 knockouts form no nephric ducts","GDNF arrests ureteric bud growth in vitro","p53 knockout mice lack both their kidneys","WT1 mutant mice form normal ureteric buds"],0,
 "Pax-2 knockout: no nephric ducts, müllerian ducts, ureteric buds or metanephric mesenchyme; death within a day from renal failure. WT1 mutants form no ureteric buds. GDNF (RET ligand, secreted by metanephric mesenchyme) promotes bud growth; GDNF knockouts have impaired bud formation. p53 knockouts have no major renal anomaly. The renin-angiotensin system is needed for normal renal growth. Human WT1 defects cause hypospadias, cryptorchidism and disorders of sex development.",EM,["c020-a05","c020-a06","c020-r12","c020-r14"]],
["Lap pyeloplasty in children","Which statement about laparoscopic pyeloplasty in children is FALSE?",
 ["Transperitoneal visibility is usually poor","Benefit over open in infants is unproven","Posterior access is hard in horseshoe kidney","Robotic assistance eases the anastomosis"],0,
 "The peritoneum reflects light, so transperitoneal visibility is better than retroperitoneal. In newborns open access needs only a small incision; laparoscopy is feasible but advantages in infants are unproven. Posterior access to the UPJ is very difficult in horseshoe kidneys. Dismembered pyeloplasty is favoured: broadly applicable, excises the abnormal segment, allows reduction. Laparoscopic repair matches open results with good cosmesis; robotics eases suturing.",PU,["c042-a01","c042-a03","c042-a05","c042-r04","c042-r03"]],
["Atopic and contact dermatitis","Which statement about genital dermatitis is FALSE?",
 ["Allergic type is the commonest contact form","Nickel is the commonest patch-test allergen","Patch tests confirm allergic contact type","Emollients are the mainstay for atopic eczema"],0,
 "Irritant contact dermatitis (direct cytotoxic effect of soaps, metal salts, acids/alkalis, solvents) causes about 80% of contact dermatitis. Patch testing confirms allergic contact dermatitis and the allergen; nickel sulfate (jewellery, belt buckles) was the commonest in the NACDG 2003 report. Atopic dermatitis: intense pruritus, asthma/rhinitis tendency; remove triggers, gentle non-alkali soaps and frequent emollients.",SK,["c059-a04","c059-a05","c059-a06","c059-a07"]],
["Dysplasia from obstruction","Which statement about obstruction-related renal dysplasia is TRUE?",
 ["Fibromuscular collars are typical","Heterotopic bone is a hallmark feature of it","Small obstructed kidneys reflect true atrophy","Partial late obstruction is its main cause"],0,
 "Fibromuscular collars (primitive ducts from abnormal peritubular mesenchyme) are a hallmark; heterotopic cartilage (not bone) may occur; glomerular cysts are not typical. Complete obstruction early in gestation is a critical determinant. Small congenitally obstructed kidneys are hypoplastic (growth failure), not atrophic; growth impairment means fewer nephrons and delayed maturation. Excess renin can occur in obstruction without dysplasia.",OB,["c040-a02","c040-r02","c040-r04","c040-r03","c040-r12"]],
["Endopyelotomy","Which statement about endopyelotomy is FALSE?",
 ["Crossing vessels hardly affect success","Strictures over 2 cm usually do poorly","Most failures appear in the first year","Bleeding may need early angiography after it"],0,
 "A crossing vessel has the most detrimental effect on success, as does high-grade hydronephrosis; excluding these improves results (CT angiography assesses vessels). For a young patient with a crossing vessel, laparoscopic pyeloplasty is the straightforward choice. Success in selected patients is 60–80%; most failures occur within a year. Long (>2 cm) strictures do poorly; access across the UPJ must be secured first. Balloon dilation: only 42% long-term success (McClinton). Low threshold for angioembolisation if bleeding.",UO,["c089-a04","c089-a05","c089-r02","c089-r09","c089-r10","c089-r11","c089-r12"]],
["Pelvic nerves","Which statement about nerve anatomy at radical prostatectomy is FALSE?",
 ["The pelvic plexus arises from T11 to L2","Bundles lie between prostatic and levator fascia","The pudendal nerve supplies the sphincter","Slow-twitch fibres give passive continence"],0,
 "The pelvic plexus carries parasympathetic preganglionic fibres from S2–S4. The neurovascular bundles run in the lateral pelvic fascia between the prostatic and levator fasciae. The pudendal nerve is the main supply of the striated sphincter and levator ani; the striated sphincter's fatigue-resistant slow-twitch fibres provide passive urinary control.",RP,["c155-a04","c155-a05","c155-a07","c155-a06"]],
["Reflexes and rhizotomy","Which statement is FALSE?",
 ["Neurostimulation gives a delayed response","The guarding reflex promotes urine storage","Rhizotomy can make a bladder areflexic","Neuromodulation alters neurotransmission"],0,
 "Neurostimulation aims at an immediate response in neurogenic dysfunction; neuromodulation alters neurotransmission (neurogenic and non-neurogenic) with a delayed effect. Guarding reflex and bladder afferent loop reflex (sacral interneurons to pudendal efferents) promote storage under sympathetic tone and are switched off (suprapontine input) during voiding. Bilateral anterior and posterior sacral rhizotomy or conusectomy converts hyperreflexia to areflexia but harms rectum, sphincters, sexual function and legs; selective motor section was meant to spare these.",NM,["c122-a03","c122-a04","c122-a15"]],
["EM and SJS","Which statement about erythema multiforme (EM) is FALSE?",
 ["Late acyclovir speeds healing of EM","HSV precedes target lesions by about 2 weeks","NSAIDs are among common triggers of SJS","EM minor resolves in weeks without treatment"],0,
 "EM minor: abrupt symmetrical fixed red papules evolving to target lesions; most follow HSV-1/2 by 10–14 days. Suppressive acyclovir may prevent episodes, but giving it after targets appear does not help; it resolves without sequelae over weeks, often recurring. Book: Stevens-Johnson syndrome (SJS) is the major form, lasts 4–6 weeks, mortality up to 30%; drugs are the commonest triggers (NSAIDs, sulfonamides, tetracycline, penicillin, doxycycline, anticonvulsants). Update: SJS/TEN are now separated from EM; mortality ~30% applies to toxic epidermal necrolysis (SJS is lower, under ~10%).",SK,["c059-a08","c059-a09","c059-r02","c059-r03"]],
["Horseshoe and Weigert-Meyer","Which statement is TRUE?",
 ["Horseshoe ascent is halted by the IMA","The upper pole orifice tends to reflux","The lower pole ureter ends in a ureterocele","Horseshoe kidneys mostly fuse at upper poles"],0,
 "The fused lower poles cross ventral to the aorta and are trapped under the inferior mesenteric artery (IMA). Weigert-Meyer rule: the lateral, cephalad orifice drains the lower pole and may reflux; the medial, caudal orifice drains the upper pole and may have a ureterocele.",EM,["c020-a04","c020-r03"]],
["Lap safety in children","Which laparoscopic safety statement is FALSE?",
 ["Monopolar cautery is safest near bowel","Lower the pressure before removing trocars","Port leaks with high CO2 flow cool the child","Open or optical camera access is safer"],0,
 "Monopolar cautery raises the risk of unrecognised bowel injury. Lowering pressure before removing trocars reveals bleeding and stops omentum/bowel entering port sites. Large CO2 volumes from port leaks lower body temperature. Blind entry risks organ damage; bladeless optical trocars or open camera access help.",PU,["c042-a02","c042-a04","c042-a06","c042-a07"]],
["Molecular response to obstruction","Which statement about the molecular response to renal obstruction is FALSE?",
 ["Fetal angiotensin acts mainly via AT1","EGF reduces apoptosis in obstruction","Renal renin rises in obstructed kidneys","MMP–TIMP balance sets ECM breakdown"],0,
 "Fetal angiotensin acts mainly through AT2 until late gestation, when AT1 grows in importance; it regulates growth, mediates fibrosis and modulates inflammation, and is altered by obstruction. Renal renin rises. EGF reduces apoptosis and growth impairment. ECM depends on synthesis and breakdown (MMPs vs TIMPs, regulated by TGF-β and angiotensin); fibrosis is universal. Epithelial–mesenchymal transformation is bidirectional and explains α-smooth muscle actin in obstruction (not part of normal glomerular development).",OB,["c040-a04","c040-a05","c040-a06","c040-a08","c040-r13","c040-r14","c040-r05"]],
["Fascia and dorsal vein","Which statement about periprostatic anatomy is FALSE?",
 ["Denonvilliers is thickest at the apex","The dorsal vein has three main branches","Three fascial layers cover the prostate","Prostatic fascia fuses with levator fascia"],0,
 "Denonvilliers fascia is densest at the base and over the seminal vesicles, thinning caudally to end at the striated sphincter. Dorsal vein: a superficial midline branch and two lateral branches. Three layers: Denonvilliers, prostatic and levator fasciae; laterally the prostatic fascia fuses with the levator fascia.",RP,["c155-r01","c155-r02","c155-r03","c155-r04"]],
["Psoriasis, Reiter, lichen planus","Which statement is FALSE?",
 ["PUVA is first choice for genital psoriasis","Reiter syndrome is linked to HLA-B27","Systemic steroids speed lichen planus clearance","Low-potency topical steroids suit genital psoriasis"],0,
 "Psoriasis affects up to 2%; genital disease is treated with short courses of low-potency topical steroids (book example: 3% liquor carbonis detergens in 1% hydrocortisone). High-dose PUVA raises genital SCC risk, so shield genitals; PUVA is contraindicated for genital lesions. Reiter syndrome (now reactive arthritis): urethritis, arthritis, conjunctivitis, oral ulcers, skin lesions after urethritis or GI infection; commoner with HIV; HLA-B27. Lichen planus: asymptomatic genital lesions need no treatment; topical steroids first; systemic steroids cut clearance from 29 to 18 weeks.",SK,["c059-a10","c059-a11","c059-a12","c059-r04"]],
["UPJ repair indications","Which statement about UPJ obstruction is FALSE?",
 ["Most neonatal unilateral HN needs repair","Intrinsic UPJO has longitudinal muscle bundles","UPJ obstruction may coexist with reflux","Kidneys under 15% function rarely recover"],0,
 "Carefully followed neonatal unilateral hydronephrosis needs pyeloplasty in only about 7%. Intrinsic obstruction: an aperistaltic segment where spiral muscle is replaced by longitudinal bundles and fibrous tissue. UPJO may coexist with VUR. Repair indications: symptoms, impaired function, stones, infection, hypertension. In adults, kidneys with <15% function are generally not salvageable.",UO,["c089-r07","c089-r01","c089-r03","c089-r06","c089-r08"]],
["S3 response","Which statement about SNM lead placement is TRUE?",
 ["S3 gives bellows and great toe flexion","S4 causes rotation of the whole leg","Urodynamics reliably predict a response","S3 lies about 13 cm above the coccyx"],0,
 "S3: levator 'bellows' contraction plus ipsilateral great toe flexion (plantar), with a pulling sensation in rectum, scrotum or vagina. (The book's keyed option says 'dorsiflexion'; the standard description is plantar flexion of the great toe.) S2: leg/hip rotation and anal clamp; S4: bellows only. S3 is located by the cross-hair technique and simple measurements; 11 cm (from the anal verge) or 13 cm from the coccyx are too far and land near S2/S1. No urodynamic or other preclinical factor predicts response: a percutaneous or staged lead trial is the best predictor.",NM,["c122-a05","c122-a08","c122-a16"]],
["Bladder development","Which statement about bladder development is FALSE?",
 ["Smooth muscle first appears at the bladder neck","The urachus becomes the median umbilical ligament","Lineage studies trace trigone mainly to bladder","Detrusor appears between weeks 7 and 12"],0,
 "Between weeks 7 and 12, connective tissue condenses and smooth muscle appears first at the dome, then toward the neck. By week 12 the urachus becomes a fibrous cord, the median umbilical ligament. Cell lineage studies show the trigone forms mostly from bladder (urogenital sinus) smooth muscle with only a minor ureteral contribution, unlike the classic theory.",EM,["c020-a07","c020-a08","c020-a09"]],
["Lichen sclerosus","Which statement about lichen sclerosus (LS) is FALSE?",
 ["It is a variant of lichen planus","BXO is its late, scarring stage","It is linked to non-HPV penile cancer","Biopsy helps exclude malignant change"],0,
 "LS shares little with lichen planus or nitidus beyond itch and genital predilection. It is a scarring disorder with pallor, loss of architecture and hyperkeratosis; the late stage, balanitis xerotica obliterans, can involve the urethra and cause stricture. It is associated with penile SCC (especially non-HPV variants) and may be premalignant; biopsy confirms and excludes cancer. Standard first-line therapy is an ultrapotent topical steroid (e.g., clobetasol).",SK,["c059-a13","c059-a14"]],
["Dorsal vein control","Which statement about dorsal vein complex (DVC) handling is FALSE?",
 ["Partial division best stops DVC bleeding","Pubic fixation of the sphincter is kept","Its proximal edges are oversewn in a V shape","The fascia is opened only superficially"],0,
 "For troublesome DVC bleeding, divide the complex completely over the urethra and oversew the end; partial division plus traction opens veins and worsens bleeding. Incise the endopelvic fascia superficially, just enough to expose the junction of the anterior apex and the DVC; keep the pubourethral component to preserve anterior sphincter fixation. Divide the complex with minimal blood loss and without injuring the striated sphincter (incontinence). Oversew the proximal edges in a V, not pulled together in the midline, which would advance the NVBs anteriorly.",RP,["c155-a10","c155-a11","c155-a13","c155-a21","c155-r24"]],
["Function decline after relief","Years after pyeloplasty, a child's differential function falls from 40% to 32% with good drainage. Most likely cause:",
 ["Faster growth of the other kidney","Recurrent UPJ obstruction","Ongoing occult pyelonephritis","Renal artery stenosis after repair"],0,
 "The affected kidney cannot raise its absolute function as fast as the healthy kidney, so relative uptake falls: a relative, not absolute, loss. Still, 20–40% of post-obstructed kidneys deteriorate: reduced nephron number causes hyperfiltration, glomerulosclerosis and decline, and without functional reserve creatinine rises as the child grows.",OB,["c040-a03","c040-r16","c040-r06","c040-r01"]],
["Incision direction","Which endoureterotomy incision direction is correct?",
 ["Proximal ureter: lateral","Distal ureter: posterior","Mid ureter: medial side","Primary UPJ: anterior wall"],0,
 "Proximal ureteral strictures are incised laterally (or posterolaterally), like the UPJ; posterior incision is used for UPJO after failed open pyeloplasty. Mid and distal strictures are incised anteriorly (anteromedially, away from iliac vessels). Left ureteroenteric strictures do poorly endoscopically (more mobilisation, poorer blood supply); open repair succeeds in about 80%.",UO,["c089-a06","c089-r15","c089-a07"]],
["Genital ducts","Which statement about genital duct development is FALSE?",
 ["Leydig cells secrete MIS","Testosterone drives wolffian ducts","Appendix testis is a müllerian remnant","Gartner cysts are wolffian remnants"],0,
 "Sertoli cells, differentiating in response to SRY, secrete MIS, which makes the paramesonephric (müllerian) ducts regress between weeks 8 and 10; these ducts form lateral to the mesonephric ducts. Leydig cell testosterone makes the wolffian ducts form the vas deferens and seminal vesicles. Müllerian remnants in males: prostatic utricle, appendix testis. Without MIS and androgens, müllerian ducts form tubes, uterus and upper two-thirds of the vagina; wolffian remnants are the epoophoron, paroophoron and Gartner duct cysts. The hymen forms at the junction of the vaginal plate and urogenital sinus.",EM,["c020-a11","c020-r04","c020-r05","c020-r21","c020-a14","c020-r09","c020-r20"]],
["Behçet disease","Which statement about Behçet disease (BD) is FALSE?",
 ["Its genital ulcers are painless","Oral plus genital aphthae suggest BD","Uveitis is part of its classic triad","Neurologic disease takes priority over ulcers"],0,
 "Classic triad: oral and genital mucocutaneous lesions and uveitis; ulcers are painful. Relapsing, remitting, with genetic predisposition and autoimmune pathogenesis; may include epididymitis, thrombophlebitis, aneurysms, GI, neurologic and arthritic disease. Exclude aphthae, syphilis, herpes and chancroid first. Book case: seizures plus a painful genital ulcer: neurology review of his antiseizure treatment comes first.",SK,["c059-a15","c059-a23","c059-r05"]],
["Fetal urine","In fetal obstructive uropathy, which finding suggests irreversible renal damage?",
 ["Urine sodium close to serum","Very hypotonic fetal urine","No calyceal dilatation","Low urine calcium level"],0,
 "Fetal urine sodium, chloride, osmolality and calcium correlate with fetal renal function; values approaching serum suggest irreversible damage (commonly quoted favourable values: Na <100 mmol/L, Cl <90 mmol/L, osmolality <200 mOsm/L). Distinct calyces help judge obstruction: no caliectasis suggests mild or minimal functional obstruction.",OB,["c040-r07","c040-r08"]],
["Failed SNM","A woman with OAB failed drugs and then sacral neuromodulation. The most logical next step is:",
 ["Intradetrusor botulinum toxin A","Add a contralateral sacral lead","Switch to bilateral S3 leads now","Augmentation cystoplasty at once"],0,
 "She should already have failed anticholinergic or β3-agonist therapy. Botulinum toxin A is as good as SNM (ROSETTA trial, 2016: slightly greater reduction in urgency incontinence at 6 months, with more UTIs and self-catheterisation). Bilateral stimulation is logical (animal data) but the only clinical study (Scheepens 2002) showed no difference for urgency incontinence; retention patients voided better volumes with bilateral leads. No prospective trial supports a contralateral lead.",NM,["c122-a10","c122-a18"]],
["Apex margins","In open retropubic radical prostatectomy, the commonest site of a positive margin is:",
 ["The prostatic apex","The posterior surface","The posterolateral side","The bladder neck"],0,
 "The anterior apical surface is hard to identify during division of the striated sphincter–dorsal vein complex; seeing the exact anterior plane avoids entering the prostate and minimises sphincter excision. Order: apex, then posterior, then posterolateral.",RP,["c155-a12","c155-r19"]],
["Reimplant principles","Which statement about surgery for vesicoureteral reflux is FALSE?",
 ["A negative on-table cystogram proves cure","Tunnel length should be 5× ureter width","Needle entry point matters in injection","Endoscopic injection has its own learning curve"],0,
 "Under anaesthesia the child cannot void, so a negative intraoperative cystogram does not show success. Extravesical, intravesical and endoscopic (bulking agent) options all exist. Every technique creates a valve that compresses the ureter with filling and contraction: a submucosal tunnel with muscular backing, length about five times the ureteral diameter. Accurate needle entry and placement determine injection success; its learning curve differs from open surgery. Laparoscopic/robotic advantages: small incisions, less pain, short stay, quick recovery.",PU,["c042-a08","c042-a09","c042-a10","c042-a14","c042-a15"]],
["Bullous and other dermatoses","Which statement is FALSE?",
 ["Untreated pemphigus usually resolves alone","Asboe-Hansen sign occurs in pemphigus","Extensive seborrhoeic dermatitis needs HIV test","Dapsone treats dermatitis herpetiformis"],0,
 "Severe untreated pemphigus vulgaris can be fatal (loss of epidermal barrier); treat with systemic steroids plus steroid-sparing azathioprine or cyclophosphamide. Asboe-Hansen sign: fluid spreads under adjacent skin from pressure on a blister. Seborrhoeic dermatitis: demarcated pink-yellow scaly plaques; extensive disease suggests HIV. Dermatitis herpetiformis: coeliac/gluten, granular IgA at the basement membrane; dapsone and gluten-free diet. Linear IgA bullous dermatosis: circumferential and linear blisters; dapsone or sulfapyridine; 30–60% long-term remission.",SK,["c059-a16","c059-a17","c059-a18","c059-a19","c059-a21","c059-r14"]],
["Pyeloplasty principles","Which statement about pyeloplasty is FALSE?",
 ["Retroperitoneal access gives more working room","The anastomosis must be watertight, tension-free","A funnel-shaped dependent UPJ is the aim","Most laparoscopic failures appear by 2 years"],0,
 "Transperitoneal laparoscopic pyeloplasty gives more working space and familiar anatomy, so it is the most used. Any repair needs a widely patent, watertight, tension-free anastomosis with a funnel-shaped, dependent UPJ. 70% of laparoscopic pyeloplasty failures occur within 2 years.",UO,["c089-a10","c089-a11","c089-r23","c089-r13"]],
["Prostate development","Which statement about prostate development is FALSE?",
 ["It requires müllerian-inhibiting substance","It buds from the urogenital sinus at 10–12 weeks","Its growth needs DHT made by 5α-reductase","Bulbourethral glands share its origin"],0,
 "Prostatic buds arise from the pelvic urethra (urogenital sinus) at 10–12 weeks, induced by mesenchyme under androgens; DHT from 5α-reductase is essential (deficiency severely impairs growth); MIS has no direct role. Prostate and bulbourethral glands come from the urogenital sinus; vas and appendix epididymis from the mesonephric duct; testis from the gonadal ridge. Androgen receptor defects cause abnormal masculinisation of external genitalia.",EM,["c020-a12","c020-a13","c020-r06","c020-r07","c020-r08","c020-r13","c020-r15"]],
["Neutrophilic and other lesions","Which statement is FALSE?",
 ["Pyoderma gangrenosum has a diagnostic biopsy","PG is associated with inflammatory bowel disease","Hailey-Hailey disease worsens with heat","Factitial dermatitis is self-inflicted"],0,
 "Pyoderma gangrenosum (PG): painful ulcers with purulent base and tissue loss; linked to IBD, arthritis, collagen vascular and myeloproliferative disease; women in 2nd–5th decades; likely autoimmune. As in Behçet, no lab test or histology is pathognomonic. Hailey-Hailey: autosomal dominant intertriginous blistering (groin, perianal) with itch, pain and odour, worse in summer; inframammary disease common, vulvar unusual; excision and grafting, dermabrasion or laser for resistant disease. Factitial dermatitis: unconscious self-inflicted lesions, linked to borderline personality disorder.",SK,["c059-a20","c059-a24","c059-a22","c059-a25","c059-r15"]],
["Neurovascular bundle release","Which statement about neurovascular bundle release is FALSE?",
 ["High anterior release lowers margin risk","The prostate is rolled, not pulled up","Release starts on the posterior surface","The posterior sphincter is muscle and fibrous tissue"],0,
 "High anterior release reduces traction on, and avoids cutting, anterior nerve branches to the cavernous bodies and sphincter, speeding recovery; but less apical soft tissue may raise the margin risk. Roll the prostate side to side with a sponge stick, with no upward traction. After developing the midline rectoprostatic plane, release the bundle from apex to base with a right angle starting on the posterior (rectal) surface, leaving Denonvilliers and prostatic fascia on the prostate. Dividing the posterior urethral band exposes the posterior sphincter complex (skeletal muscle and fibrous tissue).",RP,["c155-a14","c155-a15","c155-a16","c155-a17"]],
["SNM complications","Which statement about SNM problems is FALSE?",
 ["Infected IPGs are saved by antibiotics","Lead heating is the main concern in MRI","Switching off the IPG separates pocket pain","Lost effect may reflect lead migration"],0,
 "An infected implanted pulse generator (IPG) harbours biofilm, and infection can track along the lead to the sacrum: remove the whole device. MRI: main concern is lead heating damaging vessels or nerve roots; MRI and pregnancy are relative contraindications (book). Update: newer sacral systems are MRI-conditional for full-body scans. IPG pain: switch off; persistent pain means pocket causes (infection, waistline site, tight/loose pocket, seroma, erosion); pain that stops means output causes (unipolar sensitivity, current leak with abnormal impedance). Recurrent symptoms: check stimulation perception (wrong site, none or intermittent from migration, loose connection, high impedance).",NM,["c122-a06","c122-a11","c122-a20","c122-a21","c122-a17"]],
["External genitalia","Which statement about external genital development is FALSE?",
 ["Urethral folds form the labia majora","Both sexes look alike until 8 weeks","The genital tubercle forms from cloacal folds","Without DHT the folds do not fuse"],0,
 "Cloacal folds (5th week) meet anterior to the cloacal membrane as the genital tubercle; after cloacal division they become urogenital folds anteriorly and anal folds posteriorly; labioscrotal folds appear lateral to them. Without DHT the perineum does not lengthen and the folds do not fuse: labioscrotal folds become labia majora, urethral folds labia minora, the phallus the clitoris, and the urogenital sinus the vestibule.",EM,["c020-a15"]],
["Post-reimplant problems","Which statement is FALSE?",
 ["Early post-reimplant obstruction needs redo","Recurrent VUR may reflect neuropathic bladder","Primary megaureter has an adynamic distal segment","Secondary reflux follows high bladder pressure"],0,
 "Early obstruction after reimplant is common (oedema, subtrigonal bleeding, spasms, mucus, clots), usually mild and self-limiting; symptomatic cases present at 1–2 weeks with pain and vomiting. Observe; place a nephrostomy or stent for unabating symptoms. Distal ureteral devascularisation can cause fibrosis and recurrent obstruction. Besides technical error, recurrence often reflects unrecognised neuropathic bladder or severe voiding dysfunction. Obstructive megaureter: adynamic juxtavesical segment. Secondary reflux: neurogenic/non-neurogenic voiding dysfunction or outlet obstruction (e.g., valves).",PU,["c042-a11","c042-a12","c042-a13","c042-a16","c042-a17","c042-a18"]],
["Flaps and repairs","Which pairing of UPJ technique and indication is FALSE?",
 ["Foley Y-V plasty: crossing lower pole vessel","Spiral flap: long narrow ureteric segment","Ureterocalicostomy: failed repair, small pelvis","Dismembered pyeloplasty: crossing vessel"],0,
 "Foley Y-V plasty suits a high ureteral insertion; it is contraindicated when lower pole vessels need transposition and of little use when the pelvis needs reduction. Flaps bridge longer narrow segments (spiral flap for the longest) and are unsuitable with crossing vessels. Only dismembered pyeloplasty transposes the UPJ relative to crossing vessels. Ureterocalicostomy (to the lower calyx) is salvage after failed pyeloplasty with a small pelvis.",UO,["c089-a13","c089-a14","c089-a15","c089-a16"]],
["Infections of genital skin","Which statement is FALSE?",
 ["Fournier gangrene is mostly S. pyogenes","Erysipelas has a raised, sharp border","Hot-tub folliculitis is usually pseudomonal","Trichomycosis is caused by Corynebacterium"],0,
 "Fournier gangrene is polymicrobial (gram-positive, gram-negative, anaerobes). Erysipelas (usually Streptococcus pyogenes) involves dermis and lymphatics with a raised distinct border, unlike cellulitis. Folliculitis: perifollicular pustules in hairy areas, worsened by shaving; hot tubs/pools: Pseudomonas aeruginosa. Trichomycosis (axillary/pubic hair, hyperhidrosis): shaving, antibacterial soap, clindamycin gel, bacitracin, oral erythromycin. Hidradenitis suppurativa: follicular disorder of apocrine skin; follicular rupture (keratin, bacteria) drives inflammation, abscesses and sinuses; infection is not the initiator. Ecthyma gangrenosum: pseudomonal septicaemia with gangrenous ulcers.",SK,["c059-a26","c059-a27","c059-a28","c059-a29","c059-r06","c059-r07"]],
["Bundle decision","Which statement about preserving the neurovascular bundle (NVB) is FALSE?",
 ["The decision is fixed before surgery","A bundle fixed to the prostate is excised","Thermal energy near the bundles is avoided","A posterior arterial branch is ligated first"],0,
 "The final decision is made at surgery, even after the prostate is out: inadequate tissue on the posterolateral specimen prompts secondary wide excision. Other indications: palpable induration in the lateral pelvic fascia, or a bundle fixed to the prostate. Book: never use thermal energy on or near the NVBs. Ligate the prominent posterior arterial branch running from the bundle over the seminal vesicles to the base; the bundles then fall back.",RP,["c155-a18","c155-r06","c155-r07","c155-r23","c155-r08","c155-a19"]],
["Diuretic renography","Which statement about diuretic renography is TRUE?",
 ["T½ over 20 min suggests obstruction","MAG3 is cleared mainly by filtration","A T½ of 15 min excludes obstruction","DTPA is the preferred infant agent"],0,
 "99mTc-MAG3 is the usual agent: taken up and excreted rapidly by tubules, little filtration. Book: T½ >20 min presumed obstructed, <10 min normal, 10–20 min indeterminate. Other factors (hydration, bladder filling, diuretic timing, function, infant age) affect T½, which is less reliable in infants.",OB,["c040-r09","c040-r10"]],
["Brindley stimulator","Which statement about sacral anterior root stimulation (Brindley) is FALSE?",
 ["It suits a long-term areflexic bladder","It relies on post-stimulus voiding","Posterior rhizotomy is usually added","It needs a bladder able to contract"],0,
 "Main use: inefficient or absent reflex micturition after spinal cord injury. Prerequisites: intact pathways from sacral pelvic-nerve nuclei to the bladder, and a bladder that can contract. Electrodes go intra- or extradurally on S2–S4 roots, activated independently. Usually complete posterior rhizotomy abolishes reflex incontinence, improves compliance and avoids co-contraction. Poststimulus voiding (Jonas and Tanagho): the striated sphincter relaxes faster than the detrusor between pulse trains. Current spread can cause pain, defecation, leg contraction, erection and ejaculation; pressure rise is not coordinated with bladder neck opening. Very effective but invasive and irreversible; future versions should avoid dorsal rhizotomy.",NM,["c122-a12","c122-a13","c122-a14","c122-r03","c122-r04"]],
["Gonads and SRY","Which statement about gonadal development is FALSE?",
 ["SRY makes the wolffian ducts regress","Germ cells reach T10 level in the 5th week","SRY lies on the short arm of chromosome Y","SRY moved onto an X gives an XX male"],0,
 "SRY makes primitive sex cord cells become Sertoli cells, triggering testicular development; it does not regress the wolffian ducts (they regress without testosterone). In week 5 primordial germ cells migrate from the yolk sac along the dorsal mesentery to near T10, prompting genital ridge formation. SRY is on Yp; only about 25% of human sex reversals are due to SRY mutations (book). SRY translocated to an X gives an XX individual male characteristics.",EM,["c020-a10","c020-r19","c020-a17","c020-r11","c020-r20"]],
["Paediatric UPJO","Which statement about paediatric UPJ obstruction is FALSE?",
 ["Dysplasia is its commonest association","Crossing lower pole vessels may obstruct","Intrinsic muscle defects are one cause","Failed pyeloplasty may be redone open"],0,
 "Commonest associated anomaly: contralateral UPJO, then dysplasia and MCDK. Causes: intrinsic (interrupted circular muscle, altered collagen) or extrinsic (aberrant, accessory or early-branching lower pole vessel anterior to the UPJ). Complications: prolonged drainage, no functional or washout improvement, sometimes worse hydronephrosis/function; manage endoscopically or preferably by redo dismembered pyeloplasty.",PU,["c042-r02","c042-r01","c042-r05"]],
["Fungi and scabies","Which statement is FALSE?",
 ["Tinea cruris typically affects scrotum","Scrotal involvement suggests candidiasis","Permethrin 5% is first choice for scabies","Lindane carries CNS toxicity in children"],0,
 "Tinea cruris (jock itch) affects the inner thighs and groin, usually sparing scrotum and penis; scrotal disease suggests Candida. Dermatophytes: Trichophyton, Microsporum, Epidermophyton; post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation may persist without active infection. Scabies (as for pediculosis pubis): 5% permethrin whole body overnight, repeated after 1 week. Lindane is disfavoured: CNS toxicity in children and rising resistance. (Oral ivermectin is a common alternative.)",SK,["c059-a30","c059-a31","c059-r08","c059-r16"]],
["Ileal ureter and psoas hitch","Which statement is FALSE?",
 ["Small intrarenal pelvis bars ileal ureter","Psoas hitch may divide contralateral SVA","The genitofemoral nerve lies on the psoas","Boari flap needs normal bladder function"],0,
 "With a small intrarenal pelvis, ileocalycostomy can still be done. In a psoas hitch, dividing the contralateral superior vesical artery helps reach the ureter tension-free; avoid the genitofemoral nerve on the psoas. Normal bladder function without significant outlet obstruction is needed for ileal ureter, psoas hitch, Boari flap and reimplantation (not for endoscopic incision). If drain output rises after Foley removal, replace the catheter for some days (reflux up the stent, extravasation).",UO,["c089-a17","c089-a19","c089-a21","c089-a22","c089-a18"]],
["Anastomosis","Which statement about the vesicourethral anastomosis in open RP is FALSE?",
 ["Tension is solved by firmer traction","A Babcock clamp has cut neck contractures","Mucosa-to-mucosa apposition is the aim","Free bladder from peritoneum if tense"],0,
 "Tie the anterior suture first; there should be no tension; if there is, release the bladder from the peritoneum. A Babcock clamp approximating bladder neck and urethra while sutures are tied virtually eliminated contractures (book series). Advance bladder mucosa so the anastomosis is mucosa to mucosa, watertight and tension-free.",RP,["c155-a20","c155-a24","c155-r20"]],
["Tibial and intravesical stimulation","Which statement is FALSE?",
 ["IVES works by direct efferent stimulation","PTNS has a sham-controlled efficacy trial","PTNS is the best-studied peripheral method","Tibial implants need no large battery"],0,
 "Intravesical electrical stimulation (IVES) acts by field stimulation of Aδ afferents from low-threshold bladder mechanoreceptors (Jiang 1998). Percutaneous tibial nerve stimulation (PTNS): sham-controlled trial (Peters 2010, SUmiT); evoked potentials suggest long-term central change (Finazzi Agrò 2009); relates to acupuncture point SP6; causes ipsilateral toe flexion; FDA approved; urodynamic predictors scarce. No direct comparisons of PTNS vs implanted tibial devices or vs SNM; tibial implants are small and battery-free. Electrical stimulation in LUT dysfunction is in its infancy; almost any LUT nerve could be used; new methods and sites keep emerging, with friendlier techniques coming.",NM,["c122-a22","c122-a23","c122-a24","c122-r05","c122-r01","c122-r02","c122-a19","c122-r06"]],
["Testicular descent","Which statement about testicular descent is TRUE?",
 ["Testis is at the internal ring by month 3","It enters the scrotum by month five","Abdominal pressure plays no part in transit","Spina bifida does not raise cryptorchidism"],0,
 "The testis reaches the internal ring by the third month and traverses the canal to the scrotum between the 7th and 9th months. Abdominal pressure appears important for inguinal transit, which is why boys with spina bifida have a 23% incidence of cryptorchidism.",EM,["c020-a16","c020-r16","c020-r10"]],
["Premalignant lesions","Which statement about premalignant penile lesions is FALSE?",
 ["Bowen disease is driven by HPV 6 and 11","Bowenoid papulosis is linked to HPV 16","Verrucous carcinoma links to HPV 6 and 11","PEKMB may coexist with verrucous cancer"],0,
 "Bowen disease on mucosal male genitalia is erythroplasia of Queyrat; HPV 8, 16, 39 and 51 co-infection has been found there. Verrucous carcinoma is linked to HPV 6/11, not 16/18. Bowenoid papulosis: multiple small red papules, HPV 16. Pseudoepitheliomatous keratotic micaceous balanitis (PEKMB): thick hyperkeratotic glans plaque in older men; premalignant status debated; concurrent verrucous carcinoma reported. Book: penile CIS rarely progresses. Caution: erythroplasia of Queyrat is usually quoted as progressing to invasive SCC in up to about 10–30%.",SK,["c059-a32","c059-r09","c059-r11","c059-a35"]],
["Complex ureteric reconstruction","Which statement is FALSE?",
 ["TUU suits a patient with recurrent stones","Ileal ureter is avoided if creatinine >2","Autotransplant can bridge long upper loss","Refluxing and non-refluxing reimplants match"],0,
 "Transureteroureterostomy (TUU) is relatively contraindicated by stones, retroperitoneal fibrosis, urothelial cancer, chronic pyelonephritis and pelvic radiation. Book case (5 cm upper defect, small bladder, stones, creatinine >2 mg/dL): ureteroureterostomy, Boari flap, TUU and ileal ureter all unsuitable, so autotransplantation. In adults, refluxing and non-refluxing reimplants preserve function equally. About 25% renal function is needed to keep a UPJ or ureteral repair open.",UO,["c089-a20","c089-a12","c089-r17","c089-r22"]],
["Bleeding and rectal injury","Which statement about complications of open RP is FALSE?",
 ["Hypotension needing blood is just observed","Omentum is interposed after rectal repair","Pelvic haematoma can cause neck contracture","Re-exploration lowers incontinence risk"],0,
 "Patients needing acute transfusion for hypotension after RP should be re-explored to evacuate the pelvic haematoma, lowering the risk of bladder neck contracture and incontinence. After repairing a rectal injury, interpose omentum between the rectal closure and the anastomosis to prevent a rectourethral fistula.",RP,["c155-a22","c155-a23","c155-r25"]],
["Cutaneous malignancy","Which statement is FALSE?",
 ["EPD has another cancer in over 60%","Radiotherapy may transform verrucous cancer","Classic KS favours the lower limbs","Sézary syndrome is leukaemic CTCL"],0,
 "Extramammary Paget disease (EPD): intraepithelial adenocarcinoma of apocrine sites; another underlying cancer in 10–30% (urethral, bladder, rectal, apocrine). Verrucous carcinoma: slow, exophytic, low-grade, rarely metastasises; uncircumcised glans/prepuce; local excision; primary radiotherapy relatively contraindicated (anaplastic transformation). Kaposi sarcoma: classic type on lower limbs, genitalia seldom; in AIDS a solitary genital lesion may be first; transplant-associated KS may resolve with altered immunosuppression; neoplastic vs hyperplastic debated. Cutaneous T-cell lymphoma: pruritus mimicking benign dermatoses, then plaques, ulcers, tumours; blood involvement = Sézary syndrome.",SK,["c059-a33","c059-a34","c059-a36","c059-a37","c059-r12","c059-r17"]],
["Incontinence and sparing","Which statement about continence and tissue sparing in open RP is TRUE?",
 ["Leakage is mostly sphincter deficiency","Seminal vesicle sparing improves continence","Bladder neck sparing reduces stricture rate","Seminal vesicle sparing has caused abscesses"],0,
 "Post-RP incontinence is usually intrinsic sphincter deficiency. Book: seminal vesicle sparing did not improve continence, potency or margins, with no reported pelvic abscess; bladder neck sparing did not improve continence, potency, margins or strictures. Complete seminal vesicle excision is recommended for cancer control. Note: some meta-analyses report better early continence with bladder neck preservation.",RP,["c155-a25","c155-a26","c155-a27","c155-r15"]],
["Retrocaval and conduits","Which statement is FALSE?",
 ["Conduit hydronephrosis means obstruction","Retrocaval ureter is a venous anomaly here","Repair places the ureter ventral to the IVC","Ureteric strictures need cancer excluded"],0,
 "Most patients with long-term conduits have some non-obstructive hydronephrosis. Retrocaval ureter results from persistence of the posterior cardinal veins; repair transects the ureter and relocates it ventral to the IVC. Always rule out malignancy in a ureteral stricture.",UO,["c089-r18","c089-a09","c089-r14","c089-r16"]],
["Benign genital lesions","Which statement about benign genital lesions is FALSE?",
 ["Zoon balanitis is common after circumcision","Pearly penile papules are angiofibromas","Fordyce angiokeratomas may bleed","Eruptive seborrhoeic keratoses signal cancer"],0,
 "Zoon (plasma cell) balanitis affects uncircumcised men from the 3rd decade; biopsy shows reactive epithelium with dermal plasma cells; exclude SCC and EPD; circumcision prevents and cures most cases (steroids or laser if refused). Pearly penile papules are angiofibromas like facial lesions of tuberous sclerosis. Angiokeratomas of Fordyce: 1–2 mm red-purple ectasias that may bleed. Skin tags (acrochordons) must be told from Birt-Hogg-Dubé fibrofolliculomas. Seborrhoeic keratoses spare mucosa, palms and soles; abrupt eruption (Leser-Trélat) marks internal malignancy. Epidermoid cysts are common in the scrotum.",SK,["c059-a38","c059-a39","c059-a40","c059-a41","c059-a42","c059-r10","c059-r13"]],
["Potency after RP","Which statement about potency after open RP is FALSE?",
 ["An RCT showed sural grafts aid potency","Potency rose from 42% to 73% by 1 year","Sural grafts are laid in reversed position","Age and baseline potency affect recovery"],0,
 "A randomised study of sural nerve grafting when a bundle was sacrificed showed no difference; with bilateral grafts, 26% had full erections. Grafts are placed reversed (proximal to distal). Recovery depends on age, preoperative potency and bundle preservation; in one study 42% potent at 3 months, 73% at 1 year. Book: if PDE5 inhibitors are used, on-demand dosing is preferred to daily (trials such as REINVENT found no advantage for nightly dosing).",RP,["c155-a28","c155-a29","c155-r10","c155-r11","c155-r12","c155-r21"]],
["Retroperitoneal fibrosis","Which statement about retroperitoneal fibrosis (RPF) is FALSE?",
 ["Biopsy rarely helps exclude malignancy","Many cases are immune-mediated aortitis","Steroids are the usual first treatment","Raised ESR predicts a steroid response"],0,
 "Malignant RPF is often indistinguishable from idiopathic RPF; only biopsy showing tumour islands identifies it. Most cases are immune-mediated aortitis (many now recognised as IgG4-related disease). CT: soft tissue around the aorta and IVC effacing the planes between them (perianeurysmal fibrosis if aneurysm). Steroids first, more likely to help with active inflammation (high ESR, leukocytosis, lymphocytes on biopsy); also azathioprine, cyclophosphamide, ciclosporin, colchicine, tamoxifen (rituximab is used in IgG4 disease).",UO,["c089-a08","c089-a23","c089-r19","c089-r20","c089-r21"]],
["Late complications","Which statement about complications of open RP is FALSE?",
 ["Inguinal hernia follows in under 2%","Hernias after RP are usually indirect","Bladder neck contracture is under 10%","Rectal injury rates have fallen sharply"],0,
 "15–20% develop an inguinal hernia, usually indirect. Bladder neck contractures occur in <10%. Of complication rates, only rectal injury has changed dramatically.",RP,["c155-r05","c155-r09","c155-a30"]],
["Timing and salvage","Which statement is FALSE?",
 ["Salvage RP keeps potency in most men","Wait 6–8 weeks after biopsy for surgery","Wait 3 months after TURP for surgery","Salvage RP needs truly localised disease"],0,
 "Defer RP 6–8 weeks after biopsy and 3 months after TURP. Salvage RP only for unequivocally localised cancer; complications: incontinence 50%, anastomotic stricture 24%, near-universal erectile dysfunction, about 45% recurrence at 5 years.",RP,["c155-r16","c155-r13","c155-r14"]],
["Lymphadenectomy and endopelvic fascia","Which step in open RP and pelvic lymphadenectomy is correct?",
 ["Clip pudendal perforators at the fascia","Ligate the obturator vein routinely","Clear lymphatics over the external iliac","Keep the hypogastric veins unexposed"],0,
 "Obturator artery and vein are skeletonised but left unless bleeding; dissection goes down to the pelvic floor, exposing the hypogastric veins. Preserve the lymphatics on the external iliac artery (they drain the leg). On extending the endopelvic incision toward the puboprostatic ligaments, clip small pudendal branches perforating the pelvic muscle to avoid coagulation injury to the pudendal artery and nerve beneath.",RP,["c155-a08","c155-a09","c155-r17"]],
["Perineal prostatectomy","Which statement about radical perineal prostatectomy is FALSE?",
 ["Lowsley traction helps rectourethralis cut","Leg neurapraxia is usually transient only","Rectal injury occurs in about 1% to 5%","Rectal manipulation is avoided after it"],0,
 "Upward traction on the Lowsley tractor while dividing the rectourethralis tents the rectum and causes injury; an assistant should press gently downward, and traction waits until after division. Rectal injury: 1–5% in most series (one 11%); repaired at once the operation proceeds; unrecognised it can form a fistula. Lower limb sensory neurapraxia ~2% (one series 25%), more than retropubic, almost always transient; motor deficit rare. Rapid diet, early walking, most home by day 2; no rectal manipulation. A red rubber catheter guides the anterior sutures; the Foley goes in only after they are tied; interrupted sutures; tennis-racquet neck reconstruction if needed.",RP,["c155-a31","c155-a32","c155-a33","c155-r26","c155-a34"]],
]
